r structured reconciliation log fields | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Structured log field convention guideline and external publication | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 manager binary log standardization\"] --> E003[\"E003 CI lint enforcement\"]\n    E002[\"E002 cloudmanager binary log standardization\"] --> E003\n    E001 --> E004[\"E004 convention guideline + publication\"]\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| (none) → E001 | — | Root. Manager binary work has no in-strategy prerequisite; logr foundation exists. |\n| (none) → E002 | Rule 15 | Same repo, different binary / different area, no shared artifacts → parallel with E001. Merge coordination is a risk note, not a dependency. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 12 (consumer ordering) | The CI lint rule's deliverable is an *enforcing* gate: it can only be completed (rule active, CI green) once the manager binary's call sites conform. Enabling it against the non-conforming baseline fails CI on every build. This is a true completion ordering on the conformance state, not a merge-conflict coordination risk. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (consumer ordering) | Same as above for the cloudmanager binary — the repo-wide gate consumes the conformance state of both conversion epics. |\n| E001, E002, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy; docs describe what was built. Edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR1 — convert message-embedded identity to structured `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001, E002 | Manager-binary portion in E001; cloudmanager-binary portion in E002 (shared reconciler already emits name/namespac