{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-2036d8f2-0d16-44a4-a449-3365a942cd72","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-88cc52840a2004e3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-3k43c7al/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Cloudmanager Binary Reconciliation Log Standardization\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic applies the same structured-log standardization to the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary, which handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern, so they already receive `name` and `namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger. The remaining work is converting any message-embedded resource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs and adding `resourceKind` as a structured field, so cloudmanager logs are queryable with the same cross-controller queries as the manager binary.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Audit `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine), producing a census of call sites embedding resource identity in message strings.\n- Convert every audited call site from message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`).\n- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all three cloudmanager controllers' reconciliation error logs (name/namespace are already provided by the shared reconciler's request logger).\n- Handle cluster-scoped resources (the infrastructure engine CRDs are cluster-scoped): emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or empty — never a spurious value.\n- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via a test logger for each cloudmanager controller.\n- Perform the backwards-compatibility verification from the strategy's NFR for the cloudmanager log consumers (e.g., CloudWatch): before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error, confirming structured fields appear and the consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n- Given any cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs, measured by a static-analysis census over the cloudmanager binary returning zero remaining embedded-identity `log.Error` calls.\n- Given a cluster-scoped infrastructure resource is reconciled and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` and does not emit a spurious namespace value, measured by unit test.\n- Given a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error before and after the change, when the log outputs are compared, then the structured fields appear and the log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes, measured by the recorded before/after comparison.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR5 (P0) — cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`\n- HLR1 (P0) — message-embedded identity converted to structured `name`/`namespace` (cloudmanager-binary portion)\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Cloudmanager is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), so impact scope is narrower than the manager binary; whether it ships in the same release or a subsequent one is a PM scheduling decision that does not change this epic's content.\n- Text-based alert rules in cloudmanager log consumers (e.g., CloudWatch) that rely on message-string patterns must be identified with SRE/Support before merge — coordination tracked here as part of the backwards-compatibility verification.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":68392,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d8f917f8525c149","agent_id":"a50e633e2652d51bc","session_id":"8da6b11c-1205-4d00-b52a-b6889016afe4","uuid":"31bb1b1d-b347-4cc9-9a06-59271c119e64","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:18:36.970Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010 into epics"}
