{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9dc557e36c11a2f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Assessment (Step 0)\n15\t\n16\tThe strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator) and a single\n17\timplementation team (ODH Operator team), and most of its scope is mechanical log-call\n18\tconversion with high AI implementability. However, the below-threshold escape condition\n19\t**\"multiple distinct work streams\"** applies: the scope spans (a) code implementation\n20\tacross two architecturally distinct binaries (manager and cloudmanager), (b) a repo-wide\n21\tCI enforcement deliverable, and (c) substantive documentation authoring — a new\n22\tcontribution guideline topic plus publication for external operator adoption (P1 HLR8),\n23\twhich is new content authored in a separate review cycle, not an incidental doc touch-up.\n24\tFull decomposition (Steps 1–8) was therefore executed.\n25\t\n26\tCheck 2 (documentation only) does not apply — the strategy requires code changes.\n27\t\n28\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n29\t\n30\tThe strategy contains a non-empty Staff Engineer / SME Input section; it takes precedence\n31\tover the AI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n32\t\n33\t| # | AI-generated text | SME correction (applied) |\n34\t|---|------------------|--------------------------|\n35\t| 1 | \"~16 component action files\" | Exactly 15 action files across 4 tiers: 8 component (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`) + 3 service (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`) + 1 DSC (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`) + 3 module (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`) |\n36\t| 2 | Architecture description omitted the module controller tier | Module controller with 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) and its 3 action files are in scope (HLR6, HLR4) |\n37\t| 3 | (field naming unspecified in AI text) | camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) matching controller-runtime practice |\n38\t| 4 | (cloudmanager treatment) | cloudmanager uses the shared reconciler and already gets `name`/`namespace`; it needs only `resourceKind` addition and embedded-identity conversion |\n39\t| 5 | Open question \"how many component action files exist (~16?)\" | Resolved by SME (8/8 components have exactly one action file) — treated as answered, not an unknown; excluded from Investigation evaluation |\n40\t\n41\t## Component Mapping\n42\t\n43\t`rhods-operator` is not a canonical RHAI Jira component name. The closest canonical\n44\tcomponent for the RHOAI platform operator (owned by the ODH Operator team) is\n45\t**OpenShift AI Productization** — used for all code epics. The docs-authoring epic uses\n46\tcanonical component **Documentation**. The architecture context file\n47\t`rhods-operator.md` (rhoai-3.6-ea.1) confirms the component is in the platform and\n48\tcorroborates the strategy's premises: controller-runtime/logr usage, the 8 component\n49\tcontrollers, the Auth/Gateway/Monitoring service controllers, the DSCInitialization\n50\treconciler, the 15-action-file architecture, and the cloudmanager binary with its\n51\tAWSKubernetesEngine / AzureKubernetesEngine / CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine controllers.\n52\t\n53\t## Epic List\n54\t\n55\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n56\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n57\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager binary reconciliation log standardization | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n58\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager binary reconciliation log standardization | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | CI lint enforcement for structured reconciliation log fields | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Structured log field convention guideline and external publication | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n61\t\n62\t## Dependency DAG\n63\t\n64\t```mermaid\n65\tgraph TD\n66\t    E001[\"E001 manager binary log standardization\"] --> E003[\"E003 CI lint enforcement\"]\n67\t    E002[\"E002 cloudmanager binary log standardization\"] --> E003\n68\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 convention guideline + publication\"]\n69\t    E002 --> E004\n70\t    E003 --> E004\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\t## DAG Justification\n74\t\n75\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n76\t|------|------|-----------|\n77\t| (none) → E001 | — | Root. Manager binary work has no in-strategy prerequisite; logr foundation exists. |\n78\t| (none) → E002 | Rule 15 | Same repo, different binary / different area, no shared artifacts → parallel with E001. Merge coordination is a risk note, not a dependency. |\n79\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 12 (consumer ordering) | The CI lint rule's deliverable is an *enforcing* gate: it can only be completed (rule active, CI green) once the manager binary's call sites conform. Enabling it against the non-conforming baseline fails CI on every build. This is a true completion ordering on the conformance state, not a merge-conflict coordination risk. |\n80\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (consumer ordering) | Same as above for the cloudmanager binary — the repo-wide gate consumes the conformance state of both conversion epics. |\n81\t| E001, E002, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy; docs describe what was built. Edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n82\t\n83\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n84\t\n85\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n86\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n87\t| HLR1 — convert message-embedded identity to structured `name`/`namespace` | P0 | E001, E002 | Manager-binary portion in E001; cloudmanager-binary portion in E002 (shared reconciler already emits name/namespace there, so E002's embedded-identity conversion is smaller). Verification instrument (CI lint rule / static analysis) delivered by E003; the conversion epics verify completeness via their own static-analysis census, so E003 is not a P0 prerequisite. |\n88\t| HLR2 — DSCI migration to standard key names | P0 | E001 | DSCI controller lives in the manager binary. |\n89\t| HLR3 — service controller `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | All 5 service controllers are manager-binary controllers. |\n90\t| HLR4 — action file fields across 4 tiers (15 files) | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files are in the manager binary. |\n91\t| HLR5 — cloudmanager controller fields | P0 | E002 | Dedicated cloudmanager epic (distinct binary, narrower scope). |\n92\t| HLR6 — module controller + 8 module handler fields | P0 | E001 | Module controller tier added per SME override #2. |\n93\t| HLR7 — DSC + component controller `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 | **bundled**: `resourceKind` for DSC and the 8 component controllers edits the very `log.Error` call sites that HLR1's embedded-identity conversion touches, in the same controllers, in the same pass. A separate P1 epic would double-touch the same call sites and duplicate the call-site audit; the work is not separately schedulable and ships no independent user value (it is a queryability enhancement of the same log surface). Priority label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n94\t| HLR8 — document convention and publish for external adoption | P1 | E004 | docs-authoring priority exception: strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1. |\n95\t\n96\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n97\t\n98\t## Open Question Disposition (Step 3)\n99\t\n100\tNo Investigation epics. Each open question was evaluated against \"does the answer change\n101\twhich downstream epics exist or what they do?\":\n102\t\n103\t| Open question | Disposition |\n104\t|---------------|-------------|\n105\t| Extra fields beyond name/namespace/kind (PM / Observability) | No structural change — the required field set is fixed by the HLRs; extra fields would be an additive extension recorded in the guideline. Captured as an open question + AC on E004. |\n106\t| Which log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns (SRE / Support) | No structural change — resolved during the log-consumer regression validation; captured as an AC/risk on E001 and E002 (fix-and-retry, same work either way). |\n107\t| Cloudmanager same release or subsequent (PM / ODH Operator) | Scheduling decision, not structural — E002 exists and does the same work regardless of target release. Noted in Health Warnings. |\n108\t| Actual percentage of embedded-identity calls (ODH Operator) | No structural change — the census is the first step of E001/E002's audit; conversion proceeds the same way regardless of the percentage. AC on E001/E002. |\n109\t\n110\t## Health Warnings\n111\t\n112\t- **E001 above one sprint (managed)**: the strategy's effort arithmetic puts E001 at\n113\t  ~1.25–1.5 sprints (audit ~0.5 + manager conversion ~0.75–1). It is kept as ONE logical\n114\t  change — a single mechanical pass over one surface (reconciliation-error `log.Error`\n115\t  call sites in the manager binary) with one shared audit census; splitting by controller\n116\t  tier would duplicate the audit and fragment the pass. **Re-split trigger**: if the\n117\t  call-site census materially exceeds the SME-estimated volume, or the conversion exceeds\n118\t  one sprint of effort, split E001 along controller-tier boundaries (DSC+DSCI /\n119\t  component controllers + component action files / service + module tiers), keeping the\n120\t  shared audit checklist with the first split epic.\n121\t- **Scheduling open question**: whether cloudmanager ships in the same release as the\n122\t  manager work (PM-owned) may shift E002's target release but does not change epic\n123\t  structure. Revisit at planning.\n124\t- **Convention scope open question**: the PM/Observability decision on optional extra\n125\t  fields may expand E004's guideline content; the required core fields are fixed by the\n126\t  HLRs, so this is non-blocking.\n127\t- **Architecture context tier-count discrepancy**: the architecture context summarizes\n128\t  \"three controller tiers\" while the SME input specifies five (adding the module\n129\t  controller tier and DSCI). The SME input takes precedence (Step 1.5); the context file\n130\t  corroborates the individual controllers (DSCInitializationReconciler, service\n131\t  controllers, component CRDs), so this is a summarization difference, not a\n132\t  contradiction.\n133\t- **Strategy-level process gates pending**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture\n134\t  Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, etc.) are \"Pending human review\" —\n135\t  strategy sign-off gates outside this decomposition's scope.\n136\t- **SRE/Support coordination**: identification of text-based alert rules that rely on\n137\t  message-string patterns (Loki, CloudWatch consumers) is required before merge of the\n138\t  conversion work — tracked as an AC/risk on E001 and E002, not a DAG dependency.\n139\t\n140\t## Tiered Delivery\n141\t\n142\tNot applicable. The only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team adoption\n143\t(Loki Operator, Tempo Operator), which is voluntary per platform policy overlay 0008 —\n144\tRHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies. No in-scope\n145\twork is gated on it: the strategy delivers full value through unilateral publication of\n146\tthe convention (E004), so there is no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split. Publication includes filing\n147\ttracking issues in the COO projects; adoption itself is explicitly out of scope.\n148\t"}]},"