{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc8de26193f55e7f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n3\ttitle: Manager binary reconciliation log standardization\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: -1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 6\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Manager Binary Reconciliation Log Standardization\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic converts message-embedded resource identity into structured key-value pairs and standardizes structured field names across every controller tier and action file of the rhods-operator manager binary. Reconciliation error logs currently embed resource name and namespace in human-readable message strings (approximately 75% of log calls per the SME audit), which prevents operators from querying logs by resource name, namespace, or kind. After this epic, every reconciliation error log in the manager binary emits `name`, `namespace` (for namespaced resources), and `resourceKind` as structured fields using camelCase keys aligned with controller-runtime conventions, and the DSCInitialization controller no longer uses its non-standard `Request.Name` / `DSCInitialization` key names.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the manager binary, producing a tracked census/checklist of call sites embedding resource identity in message strings (confirms the SME-estimated ~75% figure).\n38\t- Convert every audited call site from message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs: `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", obj.Name, \"namespace\", obj.Namespace, \"resourceKind\", kind)`.\n39\t- Migrate the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller from non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to the standard camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`).\n40\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all manager-binary controllers: the DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller, the 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI), the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator), the DSCI controller, and the 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup).\n41\t- Update all 15 action files across the 4 tiers to emit `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields:\n42\t  - 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`)\n43\t  - 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`)\n44\t  - 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`)\n45\t  - 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`)\n46\t- Handle cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or empty — never a spurious value.\n47\t- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via a test logger for each controller tier and action file tier.\n48\t- Perform the backwards-compatibility verification from the strategy's NFR: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error, confirming structured fields appear and existing log consumers (e.g., Loki) ingest both formats without configuration changes.\n49\t- Document the DSCI key-name migration in release notes with before/after query examples.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- Given any manager-binary reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), measured by a static-analysis census over the manager binary returning zero remaining embedded-identity `log.Error` calls.\n54\t- Given the DSCInitialization controller's reconciliation error logs, when the code is updated, then all structured field names use the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) and a codebase grep confirms no references to `Request.Name` or `DSCInitialization` as log field names remain, measured by unit test asserting the standard field names.\n55\t- Given a service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `resourceKind` as a structured field, measured by unit test parsing test logger output.\n56\t- Given an action file error log across any of the 4 tiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module — 15 files total), when the code is updated, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`, measured by unit tests asserting all three fields across all 15 action files.\n57\t- Given the module controller or any of its 8 module handlers encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`, measured by unit test on test logger output.\n58\t- Given the DSC controller or any of the 8 component controllers encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `resourceKind`, measured by unit test on test logger output.\n59\t- Given a cluster-scoped resource (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) is reconciled and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` and does not emit a spurious namespace value, measured by unit test.\n60\t- Given a sample reconciliation error before and after the change, when the log outputs are compared, then the structured fields appear and the log consumer ingests both formats without configuration changes, measured by the recorded before/after comparison.\n61\t- The DSCI key-name migration is documented in release notes with before/after query examples, measured by the release-note content being present and reviewed.\n62\t\n63\t## HLR Traceability\n64\t\n65\t- HLR1 (P0) — message-embedded identity converted to structured `name`/`namespace` (manager-binary portion)\n66\t- HLR2 (P0) — DSCI controller migrated to standard key names\n67\t- HLR3 (P0) — service controller `resourceKind`\n68\t- HLR4 (P0) — action file fields across all 4 tiers (15 files)\n69\t- HLR6 (P0) — module controller and 8 module handler fields\n70\t- HLR7 (P1, bundled) — DSC and component controller `resourceKind`; bundled because it edits the same `log.Error` call sites as the HLR1 conversion in the same controllers and the same pass — not separately schedulable\n71\t\n72\t## Risks / Notes\n73\t\n74\t- Some error paths may be missed during the initial audit (especially action files) — mitigated by the static-analysis census and checklist tracked in this epic's scope, and by the downstream CI lint enforcement.\n75\t- Text-based alert rules that rely on message-string patterns must be identified with SRE/Support before merge and updated where affected — coordination tracked here as part of the backwards-compatibility verification; it is a risk item, not a DAG dependency.\n76\t- The migration should land in a single release to avoid long-term key-name inconsistency (per strategy risk note).\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c4a33f5d4febd9e","agent_id":"af261a6c2b9cc1ff0","session_id":"8da6b11c-1205-4d00-b52a-b6889016afe4","uuid":"286c6eee-d60d-4b9e-a1e7-0d8bf4b6e72f","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:20:52.967Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc8de26193f55e7f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
