{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bf703a4fdce07a6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E004\n3\ttitle: Structured log field convention guideline and external publication\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: Documentation\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n14\tai_signals:\n15\t  change_specificity: 1\n16\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n17\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n18\t  existing_foundation: 1\n19\t  open_questions: -1\n20\t  external_dependency: -1\n21\t  human_process_gates: -1\n22\t  repo_access: 1\n23\t  architecture_claims: 1\n24\tinvestigation_signals: null\n25\tai_implementability: Medium\n26\tai_implementability_score: 1\n27\tjira_key: null\n28\tbranch: null\n29\tgated_by: null\n30\tgate_failure_impact: null\n31\t---\n32\t# Structured Log Field Convention Guideline and External Publication\n33\t\n34\t## Description\n35\t\n36\tThis epic authors and publishes the structured log field convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development in the rhods-operator, and publishes it externally for voluntary adoption by other operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator). The guideline documents the required fields, the naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis that motivated the standardization, so that new controllers conform from the start and external operators can align their log formats. It is authored after the implementation work so the guideline describes what was actually built.\n37\t\n38\t## Scope\n39\t\n40\tContent outline (authored inside the contribution guideline):\n41\t\n42\t- Required structured fields: `name`, `namespace` (for namespaced resources; omitted or empty for cluster-scoped resources), and `resourceKind`.\n43\t- camelCase field-naming rationale — alignment with controller-runtime's existing structured log key conventions.\n44\t- The three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names, and missing `resourceKind`.\n45\t- Before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs.\n46\t- DSCI key-name migration guide with before/after query examples (for operators with existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on `Request.Name` / `DSCInitialization`).\n47\t- Guidance for new controller development: what every reconciliation error log must emit and how the CI lint rule enforces it.\n48\t- A recorded decision on optional fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category) — included or explicitly deferred with rationale (open question owned by PM / Observability team).\n49\t\n50\tPublication activities:\n51\t\n52\t- Publish the guideline in the repository's contributor documentation.\n53\t- Publish the convention for external operator adoption and file tracking issues in the COO projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator); adoption remains at their discretion per platform policy overlay 0008 (RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies).\n54\t\n55\t## Acceptance Criteria\n56\t\n57\t- The structured log field convention is documented in a contribution guideline specifying the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis, measured by the published guideline containing each element.\n58\t- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs and a DSCI key migration guide with before/after query examples, measured by the published content.\n59\t- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented logging changes (the manager-binary, cloudmanager-binary, and CI lint enforcement work), measured by a recorded review sign-off from an engineer involved in that work.\n60\t- The decision on optional fields beyond name/namespace/kind is recorded in the guideline (either included or explicitly deferred with rationale), measured by the guideline containing the decision.\n61\t- The convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are filed in the COO projects, measured by the published convention and the filed issues; COO adoption itself is voluntary and out of scope.\n62\t\n63\t## HLR Traceability\n64\t\n65\t- HLR8 (P1) — document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption\n66\t\n67\t## Risks / Notes\n68\t\n69\t- COO team adoption timing is external and uncertain; per platform policy (overlay 0008) adoption is best-effort and cannot be mandated — publication and tracking issues are the deliverable, adoption is not.\n70\t- The open question on optional extra fields (PM / Observability team) may expand the guideline's field set; the required core fields are fixed by the strategy's HLRs, so this is a content decision, not a structural one.\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c4a33f5d4febd9e","agent_id":"af261a6c2b9cc1ff0","session_id":"8da6b11c-1205-4d00-b52a-b6889016afe4","uuid":"5c36235e-9edc-450e-8c35-a411c606844e","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:20:54.939Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8bf703a4fdce07a6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
