ied consumer ordering\n38\t(Rule 12): the epic's deliverable is an enforcing CI gate whose \"zero findings, CI green\"\n39\tAC can only be met once both binaries conform — a true completion ordering, recorded in\n40\tthe DAG Justification table, not mere merge coordination. No implementation-type\n41\tparallelism violations: no konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding\n42\tepics exist (all code epics are general implementation; validator confirmed). Critical\n43\tpath 3 (E001→E003→E004) sits above the S heuristic (1-2) but is the rule-compliant\n44\tminimum for this epic set: Rule 11 mandates E003→E004, and collapsing it would require\n45\tbundling P1 CI tooling into the P0 conversion epics. No missing content-derived edges:\n46\tE003's CI failure message references the convention (whose normative content is fixed by\n47\tthe HLRs), not the E004 document artifact, so no edge is required.\n48\t\n49\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n50\tComponent/team tuples are consistent (code epics: OpenShift AI Productization / ODH\n51\tOperator team; docs epic: Documentation component). The E001/E002 split along\n52\tmanager/cloudmanager binary lines is a correct area split that also isolates the PM\n53\trelease-timing open question; it is not scope duplication — the two epics touch disjoint\n54\tcall-site surfaces. No two epics independently build the same data structure, API, or\n55\tsurface. Work-product types are properly separated: code conversion (E001/E002), CI\n56\ttooling (E003), and content authoring (E004) are distinct epics. E001's size (~1.25–1.5\n57\tsprints per the strategy's effort arithmetic) exceeds the ~2-week guideline but is ONE\n58\tlogical change (a single mechanical pass over one surface with one shared audit census),\n59\tand the summary's Health Warnings records a concrete re-split trigger (census volume /\n60\tconversion effort, with the controller-tier split lines specified) — a managed watch\n61\titem, not a defect; no split is suggeste